# What this package binds and exposes

This file is pasted into the prompt that writes the CHANGELOG entry for an
upstream dependency bump (`make update-changelog`, and the automatic update pull
request). It is the list that entry is classified against: an upstream change
that cannot be tied to something named here gets no bullet of its own, and
giving it one turns somebody else's release notes into a list of features this
package does not have. That is the single most common way this step goes wrong.
The opposite mistake is the quieter one: a change inside a crate you bind,
dismissed because its name is not here, when an item that is here runs through
it — a cipher under every message, an encoder under a display string.

**This file is yours, not the template's.** The template writes it once and never
overwrites it (`_skip_if_exists`), so `copier update` will not touch your edits
and there is no conflict to resolve later. What arrives below is a starting
point derived from the crates you answered — replace it with the real thing.

Three labels here are load-bearing, because the prompt quotes them: the
heading `## Not bound or exposed`, and the two bullet labels `Crates bound:` and
`Exposed surface:`. Keep all three verbatim — rewriting a bullet into prose
leaves the prompt's rule 2 pointing at nothing. Everything else is yours. And
prefer concrete names over categories: a model can check "does this change touch
the `SessionRecord` type" and cannot check "does this change touch state
management". Name types, functions and crates, not areas of responsibility.

**Write what a change CAN reach, never what one does.** This file is an input to
a prompt, not documentation, and the model reads it as material it may rely on.
A sentence asserting that changes to some dependency "change the bytes on the
wire" is handed over as a standing fact, so it comes back restated as a finding
about a release nobody checked it against. Phrase the same knowledge as the
question to answer — what such a change is able to touch, and how to tell
whether this one did.

That matters most for a dependency in a DIFFERENT repository that still reaches
your users — one pulled in transitively, which no crate under "Crates bound"
names. Say so here, because nothing else will: the prompt is given the compare
for the upstream repository alone, where such a dependency leaves one trace, a
version number in a manifest, and nothing about what changed inside it. So name
it, and say what it can affect — then say that the compare does not cover it, so
an entry must report the version move and stop rather than infer what changed
inside.

⚠️ **Do not tell the model to go and fetch that range.** This file is pasted
into a prompt whose client sends a plain completion with **no tools at all**, so
an instruction to run a command is one the model cannot follow and can only
appear to satisfy by inventing the answer — the same failure this section is
about, one level up. Commands for whoever finishes the pull request belong in
`CLAUDE.md`, not here.

